Derive the following The Fermi-Dirac Distribution and Fermi Energy relation from the preceding result, showing the algebra, calculus, counting, or thermodynamic identity used at each step.
\[
\overline n_\alpha
=\frac{1}{\beta}\frac{\partial\ln\Xi_\alpha}{\partial\mu}
=\frac{1}{e^{\beta(\epsilon_\alpha-\mu)}+1}
\equiv f_{\mathrm{FD}}(\epsilon_\alpha).
\tag*{[2]}
\]
Notation: \(T,k_{\mathrm B},\beta\) denotes temperature, Boltzmann constant, and inverse thermal energy; unit \(\mathrm{K, J K^{-1}, J^{-1}}\); \(\alpha,\epsilon_\alpha,n_\alpha\) denotes complete one-particle state label, energy, and occupation number; unit \(\mathrm{1, J, 1}\); \(\mu,\Xi_\alpha\) denotes particle chemical potential and single-state grand partition function; unit \(\mathrm{J, 1}\); \(\overline n_\alpha,f_{\mathrm{FD}}\) denotes mean occupation and Fermi-Dirac function; unit \(\mathrm{1, 1}\); \(E_{\mathrm F},k_{\mathrm F}\) denotes zero-temperature Fermi energy and wavevector; unit \(\mathrm{J, m^{-1}}\); \(T_{\mathrm F}\) denotes Fermi temperature, E_F/k_B; unit \(\mathrm{K}\); \(n,m,\hbar\) denotes number density, particle mass, and reduced Planck constant; unit \(\mathrm{m^{-3}, kg, J s}\).

